(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-membrane-potentials-action-potentials-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials — Physiology Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"What is the function of T-tubules in excitation-contraction coupling?","choices":["They are the site of actin-myosin cross-bridge cycling, forming the contractile apparatus that shortens the sarcomere during contraction","They store Ca2+ long-term in the same manner as the sarcoplasmic reticulum, using calsequestrin to bind and buffer intracellular calcium","They synthesize ATP for contraction via oxidative phosphorylation, functioning as an extension of the mitochondrial network within the fiber","They are invaginations of the sarcolemma, filled with extracellular fluid, that transmit action potentials deep into the muscle fiber"],"answer":3,"exp":"T-tubules are invaginations of the sarcolemma filled with extracellular fluid that penetrate, branch, and form networks within the muscle fiber, transmitting action potentials deep into the fiber to trigger Ca2+ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What structures make up the \"triad\" in skeletal muscle excitation-contraction coupling?","choices":["Two T-tubules flanking a single terminal cisterna of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, forming the site of DHP-ryanodine receptor coupling","Actin, myosin, and titin filaments interdigitating within a single sarcomere to generate contractile force","Three myofibrils bundled together by a shared connective tissue sheath called the endomysium","The junction of two terminal cisternae of the SR with one T-tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The \"triad\" is the junction between two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum and a T-tubule, where the DHP receptor (voltage sensor on the T-tubule) communicates with the ryanodine receptor (Ca2+ release channel on the SR). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the key difference between skeletal and cardiac muscle excitation-contraction coupling?","choices":["Skeletal muscle uses Voltage-Activated Calcium Release (VACR) via the DHP receptor; cardiac muscle uses Calcium-Activated Calcium Release (CACR), where Ca2+ entry through an L-type channel triggers further SR Ca2+ release","Both muscle types rely on direct mechanical coupling between the DHP receptor and ryanodine receptor, with no extracellular calcium influx required to trigger SR release in either tissue type","Neither muscle type uses the ryanodine receptor for SR calcium release; both rely instead on direct calcium diffusion through gap junctions between adjacent sarcomeres","Skeletal muscle uses Calcium-Activated Calcium Release (CACR), where extracellular Ca2+ entry triggers further release; cardiac muscle uses Voltage-Activated Calcium Release (VACR) sensed directly by the DHP receptor"],"answer":0,"exp":"In skeletal muscle, the trigger for SR Ca2+ release is voltage (Voltage-Activated Calcium Release, sensed by the T-tubule DHP receptor); in cardiac muscle, the trigger is Ca2+ itself (Calcium-Activated Calcium Release), where Ca2+ entering via a voltage-gated L-type channel triggers further Ca2+ release from the SR via the ryanodine receptor. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What protein does Ca2+ bind to within the sarcoplasmic reticulum to facilitate its storage after being pumped back in?","choices":["Troponin","Titin","Calsequestrin","Calmodulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"After a muscle contraction, Ca2+ is pumped back into the SR by a Ca2+ pump, where it binds to calsequestrin to facilitate storage, terminating the contraction. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the sarcomere?","choices":["A supporting type of glial cell found in muscle","The smallest contractile unit of a muscle fiber","The connective sheath surrounding a muscle fiber","The junction between a T-tubule and two cisternae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sarcomere is the smallest contractile unit of a muscle fiber, composed of interdigitating thick (myosin) and thin (actin) filaments. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the function of titin (connectin) in the sarcomere?","choices":["It binds Ca2+ directly to trigger contraction, functioning as a backup calcium sensor alongside troponin C on the thin filament complex","It hydrolyzes ATP to power the cross-bridge cycle, functioning as an accessory motor protein alongside the myosin head","It forms the structural backbone of the thin filament, providing the scaffold onto which actin monomers polymerize","It acts as a molecular spring providing passive elasticity, prevents overstretching, and stabilizes thick filaments centered between actin filaments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Titin is a large, springy filamentous protein spanning half the sarcomere (Z disc to M band) that acts as a molecular spring preventing overstretching, provides passive elasticity, stabilizes the thick myosin filaments centered between actin filaments, and contributes to active tension generation. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What role does tropomyosin play in the thin (actin) filament at rest?","choices":["It binds directly to Ca2+ to initiate contraction, functioning as the primary calcium sensor in place of troponin C","It forms the structural backbone of the thick filament, anchoring myosin heads along its length","It hydrolyzes ATP at the myosin head to power the power stroke of cross-bridge cycling","It covers the active sites on actin, preventing interaction with myosin heads"],"answer":3,"exp":"Tropomyosin covers the active (myosin-binding) sites on the F-actin filament at rest, preventing interaction with myosin heads; troponin (with its I, T, and C subunits, the latter binding Ca2+) regulates this covering. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which troponin subunit binds calcium ions to initiate the contraction process?","choices":["None of the troponin subunits bind calcium","Troponin C (binds Ca2+)","Troponin I (binds actin)","Troponin T (binds tropomyosin)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Troponin C binds Ca2+, while troponin I binds actin and troponin T binds tropomyosin — together the troponin complex regulates the Ca2+-dependent exposure of myosin-binding sites on actin. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Where is the site of ATPase activity located on the myosin molecule?","choices":["On the light chains only","In the \"head\" region","In the tail region","Myosin has no ATPase activity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The myosin molecule (two heavy chains plus four light chains) has its ATPase activity localized to the \"head\" region, which binds actin and undergoes the powerstroke. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What causes myosin heads to detach from actin filaments at the start of a new cross-bridge cycle?","choices":["ATP binding to the myosin head","Release of Pi and ADP from the myosin head","Calcium leaving the troponin complex","Depolarization of the T-tubule"],"answer":0,"exp":"ATP binding to the myosin head causes it to detach from the actin filament; subsequent ATP hydrolysis \"cocks\" the head (with ADP and Pi still bound), and release of Pi and ADP powers the tilting powerstroke. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Why does rigor mortis (muscle stiffening after death) occur?","choices":["Excess ATP floods the sarcoplasm after death, locking the myosin heads in a permanently activated, high-force cross-bridge state","Calcium leaks continuously from the SR after death and can never be resequestered, keeping troponin permanently activated","Troponin degrades completely after death, exposing actin's myosin-binding sites permanently and irreversibly","Without ATP, myosin heads cannot detach from actin filaments, leaving the muscle in a fixed state of contracture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rigor mortis occurs because, without ATP production after death, myosin heads cannot detach from actin filaments (ATP binding is required for detachment), leaving the muscle in a fixed state of contracture. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How is active tension (AT) calculated from measurable quantities in the length-tension relationship?","choices":["AT cannot be measured or calculated by any direct or indirect method","AT = TT + PT (total tension plus passive tension combined)","AT = TT - PT (total tension minus passive tension)","AT = TT alone (total tension, with passive tension ignored)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Because active tension cannot be measured directly, it is calculated as active tension (AT) = total tension (TT) - passive tension (PT), where passive tension is the tension required to stretch a resting muscle and total tension is active plus passive tension combined. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"At what sarcomere length is maximal active stress developed in skeletal muscle, and how does cardiac muscle typically differ?","choices":["Cardiac muscle always operates above the optimal ~2 micron length found in skeletal muscle, well past the point of maximal thick-thin filament overlap","Maximal stress occurs only at maximally shortened sarcomere lengths, where thick and thin filaments overlap completely with no remaining gap between them","Sarcomere length has no measurable effect on stress generation, since active tension depends only on the frequency of stimulation","Maximal stress occurs at ~2 microns resting length in skeletal muscle; cardiac muscle normally operates below this optimal length"],"answer":3,"exp":"In skeletal muscle, maximal active stress develops at the normal resting sarcomere length (~2 microns); stress declines at both longer and shorter lengths. Cardiac muscle normally operates at lengths below this optimal length. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the difference between an isotonic and an isometric contraction?","choices":["They are the same type of contraction","Isometric contraction: the muscle shortens; isotonic contraction: the muscle does not shorten","Isometric contractions always generate less force than isotonic","Isotonic contraction: the muscle shortens; isometric contraction: the muscle does not shorten"],"answer":3,"exp":"In an isotonic contraction, the muscle shortens (tension is roughly constant while length changes); in an isometric contraction, the muscle generates force without shortening. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What determines the speed of contraction of a skeletal muscle fiber (fast/white vs slow/red)?","choices":["The number of mitochondria present, with fibers containing more mitochondria always cycling actin-myosin cross-bridges faster than fibers with fewer","The diameter of the T-tubules, with wider T-tubules conducting action potentials into the fiber's core faster and enabling quicker contraction","The amount of titin present, with fibers containing more titin cycling cross-bridges more rapidly due to added elastic recoil","The Vmax of myosin ATPase, with high Vmax fibers cycling cross-bridges rapidly (fast) and low Vmax fibers cycling slowly (slow)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The speed of muscle fiber contraction is determined by the Vmax of myosin ATPase: high-Vmax fibers (fast, white) undergo rapid cross-bridge cycling and rapid shortening, while low-Vmax fibers (slow, red) cycle and shorten more slowly. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which fiber type has high resistance to fatigue, small diameter, high myoglobin content, and high mitochondrial content?","choices":["Neither fiber type contains myoglobin","Both types are identical in these properties","Type 1 (slow, oxidative) fibers","Type 2 (fast, glycolytic) fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Type 1 (slow, oxidative) fibers have high resistance to fatigue, small diameter, high myoglobin content, high capillary density, high mitochondrial content, and high oxidative enzyme content — the opposite profile of Type 2 (fast, glycolytic) fibers. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is a motor unit?","choices":["A group of sarcomeres arranged in series within one myofibril","The junction between two terminal cisternae and one T-tubule","A collection of muscle fibers innervated by a single motor neuron","A single denervated muscle fiber with no motor neuron innervation"],"answer":2,"exp":"A motor unit is a collection of muscle fibers innervated by a single motor neuron; all fibers within one motor unit are the same type (fast or slow). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"How do small motor units (e.g., larynx, extraocular muscles) differ functionally from large motor units (e.g., quadriceps)?","choices":["Small motor units (as few as 10 fibers) provide coarse, imprecise control; large motor units (up to 1000 fibers) provide fine, rapid control","Large motor units always contract faster than small ones, since a greater number of fibers generates a larger, faster-rising net force","Motor unit size has no relationship to control precision, which instead depends entirely on the fiber type composition within the unit","Small motor units (as few as 10 fibers) provide precise, rapid control; large motor units (up to 1000 fibers) provide coarser, slower-reacting control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Small motor units (e.g., larynx, extraocular muscles, as few as 10 fibers/unit) provide precise, rapid control, while large motor units (e.g., quadriceps, up to 1000 fibers/unit) provide coarser, slower-reacting control. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"According to the size principle of motor unit recruitment, which motor units are recruited first?","choices":["The smallest, highly excitable motor units containing Type 1 fibers; larger Type 2 units are recruited only when greater force is needed","Motor units are recruited randomly regardless of size, with the CNS selecting fibers based only on which are anatomically closest to the descending axon","The largest, least excitable motor units containing Type 2 fibers are recruited first, with smaller Type 1 units activated only as force demand increases","Recruitment order has no relationship to unit size, being instead determined solely by the fiber's distance from the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"The size principle states that smaller motor units (containing Type 1 fibers) are recruited before larger ones — weak CNS signals activate the highly excitable small units first, with larger Type 2 units recruited only when greater force is needed. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What are the two mechanisms of force summation in skeletal muscle?","choices":["Only frequency summation exists; multiple fiber recruitment produces no meaningful additional increase in overall muscle force output","Summation occurs only at the neuromuscular junction as accumulating end-plate potentials, with no corresponding mechanism operating within the muscle fibers themselves","Multiple fiber summation (recruiting more motor units) and frequency summation (increasing stimulation frequency of a single motor unit)","Temporal summation and spatial summation of action potentials at the presynaptic terminal, without any contribution from motor unit recruitment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Force summation results from multiple fiber summation (an increase in the number of motor units contracting simultaneously, i.e., recruitment) and frequency summation (an increase in the stimulation frequency of a single motor unit). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What is a \"fused tetanus\" contraction?","choices":["A sustained contraction that occurs only in smooth muscle, arising because smooth muscle fibers lack any refractory period between successive action potentials","A progressive form of muscle atrophy caused by chronic understimulation of the motor unit, occurring gradually over weeks to months of disuse","A contraction where individual twitches are no longer resolved because action potential frequency is sufficiently high that the muscle is restimulated before complete relaxation","A single isolated twitch produced by one action potential, followed by complete relaxation before the next stimulus arrives"],"answer":2,"exp":"If action potential frequency is high enough that a muscle is restimulated before complete relaxation from the previous twitch, the twitches summate and individual contractions are no longer resolved — a \"fused tetanus.\" — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is the difference between muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia as growth responses?","choices":["Hypertrophy is the rare formation of entirely new muscle fibers, typically triggered by endurance training over months; hyperplasia is the common increase in actin/myosin content within existing fibers seen with weight lifting","Hypertrophy (common) is increased production of actin/myosin and splitting of myofibrils within existing fibers; hyperplasia (rare) is the formation of new muscle fibers, sometimes from endurance training","They are the same underlying process, differing only in the medical terminology used depending on whether the change is described in skeletal versus cardiac or smooth muscle","Neither process increases force generation; both instead improve fatigue resistance by increasing mitochondrial density within existing fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hypertrophy (common, occurs over weeks, e.g., from weight lifting) involves increased production of actin and myosin with myofibril splitting within existing fibers; hyperplasia (rare) is the formation of new muscle fibers, which can be caused by endurance exercise training — both increase force generation without changing shortening capacity or max velocity. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What can cause muscle fasciculations (involuntary, visible muscle twitches)?","choices":["High plasma calcium only, since hypercalcemia is thought to raise the resting membrane potential of motor neurons closer to threshold, triggering spontaneous repetitive firing and visible twitching","Low plasma magnesium (which blocks ACh release) or low plasma calcium (which lowers the threshold potential of voltage-gated sodium channels), among other causes","Only motor neuron disease can cause fasciculations, since intact motor neurons are electrically stable and cannot depolarize spontaneously under any other circumstance","Fasciculations have no known physiological cause and are considered a purely psychogenic phenomenon unrelated to any measurable change in nerve or muscle membrane properties"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscle fasciculations result from spontaneous depolarization of a motor neuron, and can be caused by motor neuron disease, hypomagnesemia (Mg2+ blocks ACh release, so low Mg2+ promotes unwanted transmission), hypocalcemia (lowers the threshold potential, causing unplanned APs), over-exertion, or excessive alcohol. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What distinguishes unitary (visceral) smooth muscle from multiunit smooth muscle?","choices":["Both types are identical in innervation pattern, each composed of electrically coupled cells forming a syncytium that contracts spontaneously via pacemaker activity, with autonomic input serving only to modulate rather than initiate contraction","Multiunit smooth muscle is found only in the gastrointestinal tract, where it forms electrically coupled sheets of spontaneously active cells that propagate contraction via gap junctions without requiring direct innervation of each cell","Unitary smooth muscle is made of discrete, densely innervated bundles of cells that contract only in direct response to their individual innervation (e.g., vas deferens, iris); multiunit smooth muscle is a sheet of electrically coupled cells that often acts spontaneously as a syncytium (e.g., gut, blood vessels)","Unitary smooth muscle is a sheet of electrically coupled cells that often acts spontaneously (a syncytium, e.g., gut, blood vessels); multiunit smooth muscle is made of discrete, densely innervated bundles that contract only in response to innervation (e.g., vas deferens, iris)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Unitary (visceral) smooth muscle is a sheet of electrically coupled cells acting in unison, often spontaneously active (a syncytium, e.g., gut and blood vessels), while multiunit smooth muscle is made of discrete bundles of densely innervated cells that contract only in response to their innervation (e.g., vas deferens, iris, piloerectors). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What is the \"latch state\" of smooth muscle?","choices":["A brief, high-energy contraction phase lasting only milliseconds, consuming roughly 100 times more oxygen than an equivalent skeletal muscle contraction","A pathological state in which the muscle cannot contract at all, caused by permanent dephosphorylation of the myosin regulatory light chain by phosphatase","The ability to maintain force for long periods (hours to weeks) very efficiently","A rapid twitch response unique to smooth muscle, lasting only a few milliseconds before complete relaxation occurs and consuming very high amounts of ATP"],"answer":2,"exp":"Smooth muscle can maintain force for long periods (hours, days, or weeks) via the \"latch state,\" reflecting its very high energy efficiency (oxygen consumption is only about 1% of an equally-tensioned skeletal muscle). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What protein does Ca2+ bind to in smooth muscle contraction, since troponin is absent?","choices":["Tropomyosin","Calmodulin","Titin","Calsequestrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Smooth muscle lacks the troponin complex; instead, Ca2+ binds to calmodulin, which is structurally similar to troponin C and also binds Ca2+, activating myosin light chain kinase (MLCK). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Is smooth muscle contraction regulated at the level of actin or myosin, and what enzyme initiates it?","choices":["Neither actin-based nor myosin-based regulation exists in smooth muscle; contraction instead occurs continuously whenever any calcium at all is present in the cytosol, with no distinct enzymatic regulatory step required","Actin-based regulation, via troponin: the Ca2+-calmodulin complex binds directly to troponin C on the thin filament, exposing the myosin-binding site in essentially the same way as skeletal and cardiac muscle","Regulation occurs entirely at the level of the SR calcium pump (SERCA), which continuously cycles calcium in and out of the cytosol to directly time each individual cross-bridge cycle","Myosin-based regulation: myosin light chain kinase (MLCK), activated by Ca2+-calmodulin, phosphorylates the myosin regulatory light chain to enable ATP hydrolysis and cross-bridge cycling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Unlike the actin-based (troponin) regulation of skeletal/cardiac muscle, smooth muscle contraction is myosin-based: the Ca2+-calmodulin complex activates myosin light chain kinase (MLCK), which phosphorylates the myosin regulatory light chain, allowing the myosin head to hydrolyze ATP and bind actin. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Unlike skeletal muscle, what is an important source of Ca2+ for smooth muscle contraction?","choices":["Only the sarcoplasmic reticulum, exactly as in skeletal muscle, since smooth muscle plasma membrane lacks the voltage-gated calcium channels found in cardiac tissue","Smooth muscle requires no calcium at all for contraction, relying instead entirely on myosin light chain phosphorylation triggered by passive mechanical stretch of the fiber","Ca2+ entering through channels in the plasma membrane (Ca2+ action potentials), similar to cardiac muscle","Only calcium bound to titin, which is thought to release it into the cytosol whenever the filament is passively stretched beyond its normal resting length"],"answer":2,"exp":"Smooth muscle can generate Ca2+ action potentials, with Ca2+ entering through membrane channels serving as an important calcium source — similar to cardiac muscle and unlike skeletal muscle, which relies solely on SR calcium release. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How does control of smooth muscle by the autonomic nervous system (e.g., norepinephrine, acetylcholine) differ from control at the skeletal neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Norepinephrine has no direct effect on smooth muscle receptors at all, acting only on cardiac and skeletal muscle tissue through entirely separate adrenergic signaling pathways","The same neurotransmitter can sometimes relax and sometimes contract smooth muscle depending on the tissue/receptor, unlike ACh at the skeletal NMJ which is always excitatory","Smooth muscle receives no autonomic innervation whatsoever, generating and propagating all of its contractile activity purely through intrinsic myogenic pacemaker cells","It is identical in mechanism to the skeletal NMJ and always excitatory, since acetylcholine binds the same nicotinic receptor type in both smooth and skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Unlike the skeletal NMJ (where ACh is always excitatory), autonomic neurotransmitters like norepinephrine and acetylcholine can sometimes relax and sometimes contract smooth muscle, depending on the specific tissue and receptor subtype involved. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What causes normal resting muscle tone?","choices":["Complete absence of nerve signals to the muscle, which instead maintains partial contraction through an intrinsic myogenic mechanism independent of innervation","Ongoing subconscious nerve impulses (and signals originating in muscle spindles) that keep groups of muscle fibers in a constant state of partial, involuntary contraction","Voluntary continuous contraction only, requiring conscious cortical drive that ceases entirely and produces complete flaccidity whenever attention is diverted or during sleep","Muscle tone has no neural basis at all, arising instead purely from the passive elastic properties of connective tissue and titin within the sarcomere"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscle tone results from ongoing subconscious nerve impulses (and signals from muscle spindles) that keep muscle fibers in a constant state of partial, involuntary contraction; loss of nerve supply causes flaccidity and eventual muscle wasting. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 83"}]};
